# .dev.vars.example — copy to .dev.vars (gitignored) for local testing.
# `.dev.vars` is Wrangler's local-only secrets file — read by `npm run
# preview` (OpenNext's Cloudflare preview, backed by Wrangler), never
# committed, never used in production (real secrets there are set via
# `wrangler secret put`, not this file).

# Cloudflare's own published Turnstile TESTING secret key — "always
# passes," documented at https://developers.cloudflare.com/turnstile/troubleshooting/testing/
# Paired with test site key 1x00000000000000000000AA on the client side
# once a real submission form exists. Verified directly against
# challenges.cloudflare.com/turnstile/v0/siteverify: with this exact
# secret key, siteverify returns success:true for ANY non-empty response
# string (not just the documented `XXXX.DUMMY.TOKEN.XXXX` format) — that's
# what makes curl testing of POST /api/submissions possible with no real
# widget/browser challenge. Get the zero-count exactly right — a
# one-character-off secret (e.g. missing a zero) fails with
# "invalid-input-secret", a different and easy-to-misdiagnose error from
# an actual bad *token*. NEVER use a testing key in a real deployment;
# this is for `.dev.vars` only.
TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=1x0000000000000000000000000000000AA

# Any string works for local testing — this only needs to be stable
# across requests within one dev session (it salts the IP hashes used for
# vote dedup and submission rate limiting; see src/lib/voteDedup.ts). In
# production this is a real `wrangler secret put COMMUNITY_HASH_SALT`
# value, generated once and never committed or logged.
COMMUNITY_HASH_SALT=local-dev-salt-not-for-production
